    "summary": "High‑energy He’s Gone→Truckin’→The Other One suite and fierce Tennessee Jed",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a nonstop He’s Gone → Truckin' → The Other One → Spanish Jam chain with Jerry’s scorching Tennessee Jed solo and Phil’s driving bass. Playin' In The Band and Box of Rain also shine, while the soundboard source provides crystal‑clear instrument separation.",
    "review": "The March 31, 1973 show at Buffalo's War Memorial Auditorium finds the Dead in top form, especially in the marathon second‑set suite that strings He’s Gone into Truckin', then The Other One and a wild Spanish Jam before launching into I Know You Rider. Jerry Garcia rides the peaks with ferocious leads—his Tennessee Jed solo is arguably one of his most incendiary of the era—and adds blazing riffs on El Paso and the closing Sugar Magnolia. Phil Lesh lays down a \"bomb\" of bass that propels every jam, while his baritone vocal on Box of Rain blends beautifully with Donna’s soaring runs. Keith Godchaux’s piano sits perfectly in the mix, adding soulful texture without ever crowding the guitars, and Bill Kreutzmann supplies solid rhythmic support throughout, even earning a brief spotlight during the drum feature. The three excellent soundboard recordings capture this high‑octane performance with crisp clarity, making it one of the standout 1973 snapshots of the band’s improvisational prowess.",
